[THOUGHT]  
The patient presents with classic positional vertigo—sudden, brief episodes triggered by head position changes (rolling over in bed, sitting up, looking up), without associated auditory or neurological symptoms (no hearing loss, tinnitus, nausea, vomiting, or imbalance between spells). The Dix–Hallpike maneuver is positive on the right side, showing horizontal nystagmus upon supine positioning—consistent with right posterior semicircular canal involvement. While horizontal nystagmus is atypical for *classic* posterior canal BPPV (which usually causes torsional-upbeating nystagmus), it may reflect variant anatomy (e.g., apogeotropic horizontal canal BPPV) or central compensation; however, the strict positional trigger, absence of red-flag symptoms, and reproducible maneuver response strongly support a diagnosis of benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV), most likely right-sided.

[SUMMARY]  
**Clinical Impression:**  
- **Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV), right-sided**, likely involving the posterior semicircular canal (or possibly horizontal canal variant), based on:  
  • Characteristic episodic, positional vertigo (onset within seconds of head movement, lasting <1 minute);  
  • Positive Dix–Hallpike maneuver with right-sided horizontal nystagmus;  
  • Absence of red-flag symptoms (no hearing loss, tinnitus, neurologic deficits, headache, or non-positional imbalance).  

**Next Steps Recommended:**  
- Therapeutic repositioning maneuver (e.g., Epley maneuver for posterior canal BPPV or Gufoni for horizontal canal variant, tailored to nystagmus direction and laterality);  
- Patient education on particle repositioning, sleep positioning (e.g., semi-recumbent for first night post-maneuver), and recurrence counseling;  
- Reassessment after 1–2 weeks; consider vestibular rehabilitation if persistent or recurrent.  
- No urgent imaging or audiology referral indicated in absence of atypical features.

